  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Friday)\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nThe subtropical ridge will remain centered across the southeast \nUnited States through at least the middle part of next week. \nOklahoma and north Texas will be on the western periphery of the \nridge with a strong low-level thermal ridge. Above-normal \ntemperatures are likely to continue with high temperatures in the \n90s to mid 100s deg F. Bias-corrected probabilistic guidance \nindicates little temperature spread through Wednesday (e.g., the \n10th percentile is above-normal), which indicates high confidence\nof above-normal temperatures.\n\nNorthern and western Oklahoma will be close enough to stronger flow \naloft (on the periphery of the ridge known as the ring of fire), \nwhere there will be occasional chances of showers and thunderstorms \nfrom remnant convection or nearby fronts. \n\nEnsemble spread does increase by late next week into the \nfollowing weekend (September 18-20), which indicates the potential\nfor a pattern change with the subtropical ridge breaking down and\na stronger cold front advancing southward into the area. It's not\na guarantee, but there is a 40 to 70% chance of high temperatures\nbelow 90 deg F on Sunday, September 20 (40% south; 70% north). \nThe coldest end of guidance even indicates a low to medium chance \n(10 to 40%) of high temperatures below 80 deg F.\n\nMahale",
